WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.600
First a Spanish colony and then an American
one, Puerto Rico, a small island in the Caribbean,

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:12.309
defines itself as a free state associated
with the United States.

00:00:12.309 --> 00:00:21.910
An associated state whose citizens have American
citizenship but, unless they move to another

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:26.100
state, cannot vote in the presidential elections
and, even though they have a representative

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.910
in Congress, he also lacks a vote.

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:35.220
All their major issues, be it political, economic
or military lie under the federal jurisdiction

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.280
of the United States.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:41.370
Lately, there’s been much talk about this
territory, not only because in September 2017

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:45.800
they suffered the harsh impact of Hurricane
Maria, but also because a few months before,

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.210
in May of that same year, Puerto Rico declared
itself bankrupt.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:53.390
After more than 10 years of economic crisis,
Puerto Rico’s public accounts could no longer

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:54.390
keep going.

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:58.900
Because the island’s public debt surpasses
70 billion dollars and another 50 billion

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.110
in future payment commitments, such as pensions.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:05.650
Dear viewer, we’re talking about figures
that are huge for an island that barely has

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.550
more than 3 million inhabitants.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:13.080
Due to this, in recent years Puerto Rico has
begun to be known as the Greece of the Caribbean.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:18.080
Because, dear viewers, Puerto Rico is, simply
and plainly, insolvent.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:19.080
But...

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:20.930
How did they get to this point?

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:24.450
Listen up.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:34.700
Lately, there is a lot of discussion going
on, on how to solve Puerto Rico’s serious

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:38.732
crisis, and on how to stop the exodus the
island is suffering, which we saw here in

00:01:38.732 --> 00:01:39.732
a past video.

00:01:39.732 --> 00:01:43.830
But first... to address this issue, I think
we need to answer a question...

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:45.470
What on earth happened?

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:54.420
What caused Puerto Rico to be so insolvent,
what lead it to be a broke island?

00:01:54.420 --> 00:02:01.570
Well, of course the great culprit has been,
without a doubt, the Puerto Rican government

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:02.570
itself.

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:07.479
A government that has been spending uncontrollably
for decades, has had many suspicions of corruption,

00:02:07.479 --> 00:02:10.850
and an absolute disdain for production.

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:15.910
But, beyond Puerto Rico’s government...

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:19.760
Washington also holds a large responsibility
in this whole mess.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:24.590
My friends, Washington's responsibility is
so great, that it wouldn’t be unreasonable

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:30.090
to say that the federal government has been
the great driver of Puerto Rico’s bankruptcy.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:31.090
You heard that right.

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:36.730
The United States has “bombarded” Puerto
Rico with 5 policies whose results were devastating.

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:41.220
Five policies concerning trade, regulations,
salaries and welfare.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:43.340
Don’t believe me?

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:47.080
Keep listening.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:54.550
(TAX-FREE DEBT)

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:58.209
With the supposed intention of helping the
Puerto Rican economy and aiding its government

00:02:58.209 --> 00:03:02.810
to access the capital markets, at the dawn
of the Jones-Shafroth Act, Washington created

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:08.020
a triple municipal, state and federal exemption
on Puerto Rico’s debt’s interests.

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:13.030
Thus, if any US citizen purchased Puerto Rico’s
public debt, he or she was exempt from paying

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:16.290
taxes on the interest received.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:25.090
In other words, if an American bought debt
from a company, they had to pay taxes on the

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:29.530
returns obtained, but if he or she invested
in the debt of Puerto Rico’s politicians,

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:32.470
he or she didn’t have to pay anything at
all.

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:37.480
So... you can imagine that with such attractive
conditions, people would be queueing to lend

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.400
money to the island’s government.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:44.490
Easy money that local politicians didn’t
take long to spend, aimed at a single thing:

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:48.379
buying, I mean, winning votes.

00:03:48.379 --> 00:03:55.310
What better scenario could we concoct for
irresponsible politicians?

00:03:55.310 --> 00:04:03.330
(TAX ENGINEERING)

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:07.340
Against all logic, while the federal government
encouraged the island to get deeply indebted,

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:09.660
it also made things harder for the private
economy.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:10.680
You heard that right.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:15.060
In 2006, Congress abolished the so-called
section 936 of the tax code, a section that

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:19.190
provided significant benefits for the companies
that invested in the island.

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:22.870
Let's not forget, my friends, that Puerto
Rico is the poorest territory in the United

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:24.780
States.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:30.120
Well, the fact is that this incentive model
was very, very successful.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:35.150
For years it helped Puerto Rico grow a lot,
even leading it to become an incipient manufacturing

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:39.150
and pharmaceutical power.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:45.620
However, once the incentives were over, in
2006, two years before the great international

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:49.490
financial crisis, Puerto Rico began its fall
into the abyss.

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:54.189
Overnight the companies’ conditions changed
drastically...

00:04:54.189 --> 00:04:56.610
forcing investments in the island to sink.

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:57.620
Legal security?

00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:58.620
Ha!

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:05.400
But that’s not all, not at all.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:12.349
(THE MERCHANT MARINE LAW)

00:05:12.349 --> 00:05:16.900
This may be the most controversial point of
all... and at the same time the most absurd

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:17.900
law.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:21.680
The Jones Act, which was passed in 1920 and
is still in force, forbids foreign-flagged

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:25.199
vessels to transport goods between two United
States ports.

00:05:25.199 --> 00:05:29.809
Thus, a ship loaded with containers and coming
from China cannot stop in Puerto Rico, because

00:05:29.809 --> 00:05:31.789
it has to continue to the mainland.

00:05:31.789 --> 00:05:36.000
There the containers destined for Puerto Rico
are unloaded to then be loaded again on another

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.259
ship, this time one with a US flag and crew,
which is obviously much more expensive.

00:05:40.259 --> 00:05:41.360
The result?

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:46.090
Puerto Rico has the most expensive shipping
costs of all Caribbean countries, something

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:50.909
that not only hinders trade but also makes
exports more expensive and reduces the every

00:05:50.909 --> 00:05:53.610
Puerto Rican’s purchasing power.

00:05:53.610 --> 00:06:00.449
In fact, to get an idea of this law’s implications,
we should mention that even though Puerto

00:06:00.449 --> 00:06:05.009
Rico has an average income, which is approximately
half that of Mississippi, and is the poorest

00:06:05.009 --> 00:06:12.639
state in the United States, its cost of living
surpasses the state average.

00:06:12.639 --> 00:06:17.080
It’s crazy!

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:21.580
(MINIMUM WAGE AND WELFARE POLICY)

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:25.059
And if that weren’t enough…

00:06:25.059 --> 00:06:26.460
there's more.

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:31.860
The average salary in Puerto Rico is the same
as the federal minimum wage, that is, $ 7.25

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:32.860
per hour worked.

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:36.399
The thing is that, as we’ve seen, both the
development level and average income in Puerto

00:06:36.399 --> 00:06:39.779
Rico are much lower than those of the other
states of the United States, leading this

00:06:39.779 --> 00:06:46.439
policy’s distorting effect to be... enormous.

00:06:46.439 --> 00:06:53.090
See, the federal minimum wage in New York
or Illinois may affect a few people... but

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:54.830
in Puerto Rico... it’s different…

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:59.460
the federal minimum wage is equivalent to
77% of the average salary in Puerto Rico,

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:06.960
that is, the average salary of every profession
and job on the island.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:10.479
This has led to only one in three people of
working age do so.

00:07:10.479 --> 00:07:14.779
And, by the way, if this number is already
abnormally low, over a quarter of all people

00:07:14.779 --> 00:07:16.550
with jobs work for the government.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:17.550
Which means…

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:22.460
my friends… that less than 25% of the island's
population works in the productive economy.

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:26.360
On the other hand, the federal regulation
also implies an abnormally high level of social

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:28.189
benefits for Puerto Rico’s parameters.

00:07:28.189 --> 00:07:32.699
In fact, studies show that for many families,
it is more convenient to receive social aid

00:07:32.699 --> 00:07:39.710
than to work... because they income they’ll
receive is substantially higher.

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:50.740
Well folks, the result of these 5 federal
policies has been a huge crisis, a crisis

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:54.919
that has lasted more than 10 years, which
has caused – or has at least fuelled – the

00:07:54.919 --> 00:07:59.590
island’s bankruptcy and has generated a
gigantic exodus of people who leave to the

00:07:59.590 --> 00:08:03.500
continent, especially, of course the youngest.

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:11.669
As we saw here in a past video, only in the
last 10 years, 500,000, 500,000 Puerto Ricans

00:08:11.669 --> 00:08:14.259
have packed their bags and moved to the continent.

00:08:14.259 --> 00:08:16.490
That is 1 out of every 7 people.

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:19.999
There’s a lot of talk about how to solve
the island’s problems… but the question

00:08:19.999 --> 00:08:24.889
is... is Puerto Rico’s current connection
with the United States compatible with any

00:08:24.889 --> 00:08:26.409
of these solutions?

00:08:26.409 --> 00:08:29.801
Leave your answer in the comments as well
as in the survey.

